TODO — trackview.py enhancements

All five items below are implemented and verified by rendering (2026-09-17).
Line references below are to trackview.py as of that commit.

[1] [done] Honour the --out *filename* in --split mode.
    --split now uses the --out *stem* as a prefix for the per-track files
    (`--out rides.png` -> `rides_<ride>.png`, in --out's directory); without
    --out the files are just named after the GPX stem.  A line on stdout states
    the naming when --split + --out are combined.  (main(): split_prefix.)

[2] [done] Smooth the per-segment grade line in the profile panel.
    _draw_profile() now runs a centred moving average (_moving_avg, window
    GRADE_SMOOTH = 5 segments, NaN-skipping) over the grade before plotting, so
    the climb/descent silhouette reads cleanly instead of jitting on GPS noise.

[3] [done] Expose the grade-colour cap as a CLI flag.
    New `--grade-cap N` pins the grade colour range to +/-N% (instead of the auto
    p98-of-|grade| clamped to GRADE_CAP_FLOOR..GRADE_CAP_CEIL).  The cap is
    centralised in _grade_cap(); --no-map now lists --grade-cap among the ignored
    map-only flags.

[4] [done] Replace the placeholder contact in USER_AGENT.
    Dropped the dead "user@example.com" placeholder; USER_AGENT is now a clean
    product string with a comment marking where a real contact/URL can be added
    (a fake address that nobody reads is worse than none for tile rate-limits).

[5] [done] Per-track grade colouring on a combined map.
    For a combined grade map with a few rides (<= GRADE_CBAR_MAX = 4) each ride
    now gets its own colourbar at its own cap (_stack_grade_colorbars +
    _short_label), so one steep ride can't desaturate the others.  Beyond that
    cap, or when --grade-cap is pinned, a single shared colourbar is used and a
    line on stdout says so.  (--split is already per-track, one colourbar each.)

Open proposals (re-analysed 2026-09-19).  [7] (scale bar), [10] (climb ranking) and
[15] (gain-noise) are done that day (see each item's "DONE" note); the rest below are
still open.  Line references below
are to trackview.py as of that date; flag names are suggestions.  Ordered roughly
by value; each notes the cheapest implementation hook.

Colouring, the map & the panels
[6]  Extend --color-by with {speed,power}.  The four telemetry metrics are already
     read in load_tracks (t["speed"]/t["power"], as [sum,count]); add them as
     shading modes (fast / high = hot, on a perceptually-uniform ramp) so a rider
     can read pacing and effort at a glance.  Caveat: per-segment colouring needs
     the per-point value, not just the [sum,count] average the tele dict keeps, so
     speed/power/temp would have to be retained as per-point arrays the way grade
     and ele already are (line 369); absent --ext they fall back to flat.

[7] [done] A scale bar on every map.  render() already holds the Mercator bbox
     (w0,s0,e0,n0) and the chosen detail, so a labelled "0 .. N km" bar (a "nice"
     1/2/5 x 10^k length, drawn in a corner) is cheap.  A static map with no scale
     is the one thing a viewer can otherwise never read off the image.
     DONE (2026-09-19): _draw_scale_bar + _nice_m/_fmt_len put a "0 .. N km" bar in
     every map's lower-right corner (a nice 1/2/5 x 10^k of ~15 % of the width, in
     Web-Mercator metres; a white casing so it reads on a busy basemap).  --no-scale
     omits it; it is a map-only flag (ignored under --no-map).  Smoke-tested headlessly
     (no network) and confirmed on a real basemap render.

[8]  A density / "time-on-route" overlay.  Instead of one LineCollection per track,
     weight each segment by the time spent on it (or plot a 2-D histogram of the
     points) so a combined map of many rides shows *where you ride*, not just every
     route as one line - a --heat switch or a 5th --color-by mode.

[9]  Ride labels on a combined map.  A short name + distance near each start
     marker (a halo'd text label; _short_label already exists) makes routes
     identifiable without the colourbar, so a many-ride overview reads without a
     legend key.

Analysis
[10] [done] Climb detection & ranking.  Scan each ride's t["grade"]/t["cum_km"] for
     sustained climbs (a min length + min average grade), score them a
     Strava/Komoot-style "climb score" (gain x a grade-weighted factor) and list
     the top N to stats/.  "Steepest / hardest climb" is the single most-wanted
     cycling stat and needs no telemetry.
     DONE (2026-09-19): _ride_climbs/_all_climbs/write_climbs + the --climbs [N] flag
      (default 10).  A "climb" is a maximal run of segments whose *smoothed* grade
       (_moving_avg, CLIMB_SMOOTH) stays >= CLIMB_MIN_GR (4 %) over >= CLIMB_MIN_LEN
       (250 m); ranked by a climb score = gain x average grade so a long moderate climb
     beats a short steep one.  Writes stats/trackview-<range>_climbs.txt (a self-
     describing header naming the thresholds).  Pure geometry, so it runs under --no-map.

[11] More profile lines.  _draw_profile() today draws only elevation + grade.  Add
     speed / power / HR / cadence as further lines (a 3rd twin axis, or a slim
     stacked mini-panel per metric) from the same telemetry, so one figure is a full
     "ride card".  Needs the per-point arrays from item [6].

[12] Time-series / trend chart.  A per-ride line chart of distance, gain, duration
     and calories *over time* (all rides on one axis), written to output/ or
     stats/.  Makes training-volume trends and the 2019->2026 span visible.

[13] Calendar / activity heatmap.  A GitHub-contribution-style grid of the year,
     each day coloured by that ride's distance (or gain / ride count).  A --calendar
     PNG is the best "year at a glance"; reuses _ride_date() per ride.

[14] Annual / monthly summary report.  Aggregate the ride-log per year (and per
     month): total distance, gain, rides, hours, avg power - appended to the stats/
     table or a new per-year report file.  The history spans many years, so a
     year-in-review is currently only possible by hand.

Correctness & data quality
[15] [done] Elevation-gain noise handling.  _distance_and_gain() sums *every* positive
     segment (line 392), so GPS vertical jitter (a few cm) inflates the ascent; add
     a small vertical dead-band or a short elevation smoothing (as for grade, via
     _moving_avg) before gain and the profile fill, behind a flag, so gain_m and any
     climb numbers ([10]) are realistic rather than noise-driven.
     DONE (2026-09-19, fixed same day): --gain-noise M (default 0).  _distance_and_gain
     now sums the rises of a *running climb* - it tracks the climb's top and commits the
     ascent (top - base) only when a descent exceeds M metres, so cm-scale GPS jitter stops
     inflating the figure while real climbs/descents are untouched.  M=0 reproduces the
     classic "sum of every rising segment" exactly (verified: the 115-ride total is 44,533 m,
     matching a hand positive-sum).  The first cut had a 'floor' that never reset on a
     rise, so every point above the running minimum re-added its height above it - over-
     counting ascent by ~800x (a 22,007,339 m total) - and was corrected the same day.
     The wahoo_to_gpx.py copy is left unchanged (its default 0 keeps the two identical).

[16] On-disk tile cache.  contextily refetches tiles on every run; cache the
     per-detail tiles to disk (keyed by z/x/y) so re-renders and offline runs work,
     and the rate-limit pressure the fallback/backoff in _load_basemap() is already
     trying to absorb disappears.  Pairs naturally with [7]/[8].

Selection, filtering & workflow
[17] Device / sport filters.  The ride-log already groups by Device
     (_device_groups); add --device NAME / --sport NAME (from the GPX <desc>
     "Device:"/"Sport:" lines) to restrict which rides are drawn and listed,
     complementing the --since/--until date filter for mixed-device/sport folders.

[18] --top N.  Render/stat only the N longest, highest-gain, or most-recent rides
     (a "best-of" map, or to keep a huge combined map legible): a --top N --by
     {km,gain,recent} switch, applied after the date filter.

[19] Recurse / multiple input dirs.  main() does gpx_dir.glob("*.gpx") (flat, line
     956); allow nested folders (rglob) or a repeatable --gpx-dir so rides can be
     organised by year/device under subfolders.

[20] --list dry-run.  Print the rides that *would* be processed after all filters
     (names, dates, dist/gain) and stop - no basemap fetch, no image.  A cheap way
     to preview a --since/--until or --top selection.

Output & sharing
[21] Self-contained HTML / Leaflet export.  A single .html (a tile URL + the tracks
     as inline GeoJSON/SVG) that is zoomable and shareable, complementing the static
     PNG/PDF.  Note the parent repo has a separate interactive gpx_viewer.py
     (Streamlit+Folium); this would be a *static, dependency-light, single-file*
     variant (no server) - a different deliverable.

[22] Contact sheet / gallery.  Tile the --split per-track maps into one mosaic
     image (e.g. a 3x3 of the most recent rides) - a "recent rides" overview without
     opening N files; a --gallery N switch.
